---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";
import Sequence from "../../components/Sequence.astro";

const PERM = "server/src/iki_server/org/permissions.py";
const ACCESS = "server/src/iki_server/entities/access.py";
const OAUTH = "server/src/iki_server/oauth";
const SEC = "server/src/iki_server/api/security.py";

const H = {
  short: "A token only narrows what its person may do",
  how: "What a token carries, and how an agent acts for a person",
  example: "An unattended agent receiving a sample for a coordinator",
  rules: "Scopes, projects, service accounts and MCP",
  ref: "Code and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "read-seq", label: "A delegated read, step by step" },
  { id: "example", label: H.example },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      A token can do what both its scopes and its user's roles allow: a <code>write</code> token
      held by a Viewer still only reads (<Adr n="0028" />). A person can give a client only some of
      their projects, and its tokens then act only in those (<Adr n="0152" />). An unattended agent
      runs as a service account and acts for a person by delegation: every check then runs against
      both identities, their projects and the lab roles they share (<Adr n={[73, 210]} />). Each
      token's limits are read from the person's consent as it stands now, so narrowing or revoking a
      consent applies to tokens already issued.
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <p>
      A request with a bearer token carries a <code>TokenLimits</code>: its scopes, the projects it
      may touch, whether the person chose only some, and under a delegation the agent's own service
      account, <code>alongside</code>, whose roles must allow each action too.
    </p>
    <Excerpt file={PERM} start="^class TokenLimits" />
    <p>
      <code>of()</code> builds them for every token check: the token's scopes narrowed to the
      consent's, the consent's projects, and for a delegated token only the projects the service
      account can see as well. It answers nothing when the consent was revoked or expired, or the
      person or the service account was deactivated (<Path p={`${OAUTH}/access.py`} sym="def of(" />,{" "}
      <Path p={`${OAUTH}/access.py`} sym="projects & theirs" />).
    </p>

    <h3 id="read-seq">A delegated read, step by step</h3>
    <p>An unattended agent reads one sample, as the person who delegated to it.</p>
    <Sequence
      id="read-seq-dg"
      title="A delegated agent reads GET /api/v1/entities/SAM-000012"
      width={900}
      actors={[
        { id: "agent", label: "Agent", sub: "a service account's client" },
        { id: "oauth", label: "OAuth server" },
        { id: "api", label: "API", sub: "BearerAuth" },
        { id: "perm", label: "Permissions" },
        { id: "db", label: "Postgres" },
      ]}
      steps={[
        { id: "exchange", from: "agent", to: "oauth", label: "token exchange: the delegation's grant" },
        { id: "issued", from: "oauth", to: "agent", label: "the person's token, act: the client", dashed: true },
        { id: "get", from: "agent", to: "api", label: "GET /entities/SAM-000012, bearer" },
        { id: "limits", from: "api", to: "perm", label: "limits from the consent now; alongside: the bot" },
        { id: "filter", from: "perm", to: "db", label: "readable projects ∩ the token's ∩ the bot's" },
        { id: "exists", from: "db", to: "api", label: "EXISTS a link into one of them", dashed: true },
        { id: "answer", from: "api", to: "agent", label: "200 with the entity, or 404", dashed: true },
      ]}
    >
      <div slot="exchange"><p>RFC 8693 token exchange, with Iki's own <code>delegation</code> subject-token type. The client must be allowed to act for people, which is off by default; a delegation lasts at most 90 days and only people can give one (<Adr n="0073" />).</p><p><Path p={`${OAUTH}/exchange.py`} sym="DELEGATION =" />, <Path p={`${OAUTH}/services.py`} sym="MAX_DELEGATION_DAYS = 90" />, <Path p={`${OAUTH}/services.py`} sym="Only people can let an agent act for them." /></p></div>
      <div slot="issued"><p>The response names the client as the actor in <code>act</code>, so what the agent does is recorded as the person's, through the agent (<Adr n={[72, 73]} />).</p><p><Path p={`${OAUTH}/exchange.py`} sym='token["act"]' /></p></div>
      <div slot="get"><p>The API authenticates the bearer token on every request and attaches its limits to the user.</p><p><Path p={SEC} sym="class BearerAuth" /></p></div>
      <div slot="limits"><p>A delegated token's projects are the person's choice intersected with what the service account can see.</p><p><Path p={`${OAUTH}/access.py`} sym="projects & theirs" /></p></div>
      <div slot="filter"><p>Without the <code>read</code> scope the caller sees no projects at all. Org Admins skip the membership filter for seeing projects, but reading data still needs a role.</p><p><Path p={PERM} sym="def visible_projects(" />, <Path p={ACCESS} sym="my_role__isnull=False" /></p></div>
      <div slot="exists"><p>An entity is readable when it has a project link into a readable project (<Adr n="0035" />).</p><p><Path p={ACCESS} sym="def visible_of" /></p></div>
      <div slot="answer"><p>The lookup only ever searches what's readable, so an unreadable entity and a missing one answer the same 404 (<Adr n="0101" />).</p><p><Path p="server/src/iki_server/api/entities.py" sym="No such entity, or you can’t read it." /></p></div>
    </Sequence>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={3} title={H.example}>
    <p>
      A lab runs an agent unattended to receive samples. A scientist with the Editor role and the
      Coordinator lab role delegates to it, and the agent's service account is an Editor holding
      Analyst. The agent tries to move a sample to Received, which coordinators and analysts may both
      set. It's refused: each identity holds a role that may set the state, but not the same one.
      Once the service account is given Coordinator as well, the change goes through (<Adr
        n="0210"
      />). The revision names the scientist as the person and the agent's client and grant; how that's
      recorded is on <PageLink to="Revisions and history" />.
    </p>
    <p>Who may set Received on a reviewed sample, in the samples plugin:</p>
    <Excerpt file="plugins/samples/types/reviewed_sample.yaml" start="^      received:" />
    <p>
      Under a delegation, the lab roles that count are those both identities hold. The state change
      then needs one of the state's <code>set_by</code> roles among them:
    </p>
    <Excerpt file={PERM} start="^def lab_roles\(" />
    <p>
      The same rule applies to guards, so a guard testing <code>actor.roles</code> sees the shared
      roles only (<Path p="server/src/iki_server/entities/states.py" sym="if not permissions.lab_roles(user, owner) & set(roles):" />),
      and to receiving a request's tubes, drawing material and other lab-role checks. The tests walk
      it through, refusal and success, with the test plugin's sample and with a receipt: <Path
        p="server/tests/test_delegated_lab_roles.py"
        sym="def test_delegated_state_changes_and_guards_use_the_same_shared_role("
      />, <Path
        p="server/tests/test_delegated_lab_roles.py"
        sym="def test_receiving_requires_a_shared_service_role_and_records_both_identities("
      />.
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="Scopes" open>
        <p>Each action maps to one scope (<Path p={PERM} sym="ACTION_SCOPES" />). The scopes a consent can grant:</p>
        <Excerpt file={`${OAUTH}/scopes.py`} start="^SCOPES: dict" />
        <p>
          Webhooks have a scope of their own because they send data out of Iki. Exporting or importing
          a bundle, and curating a registry, need <code>admin</code>.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Project-limited tokens">
        <p>
          A token the person gave only some projects acts only in them: a project outside them answers
          404, as one the person can't see would. It takes no action on the organization, its teams or
          its registries, and those refusals answer 403 with the code <code>token_limited_to_projects</code>{" "}
          (<Adr n="0152" />, <Path p={PERM} sym="def beyond_projects(" />). Event streams, the inbox and
          webhooks an app added apply the same limits: <PageLink to="What events disclose" />.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Service accounts and delegation">
        <p>
          An Org Admin makes a service account and its client credentials. Its own tokens act as the
          service account. To act for a person, an Org Admin must allow the credential to, and the
          person delegates to it with scopes, projects and an end date; the credential then exchanges
          the delegation for the person's token (<Adr n={[27, 73]} />, <Path
            p={`${OAUTH}/services.py`}
            sym="def delegate("
          />, <Path p={`${OAUTH}/exchange.py`} sym="GRANT_TYPE" />).
        </p>
        <p>
          Every check then runs against both: the person's roles and the service account's, the
          person's projects and the service account's, its teams and theirs, and the lab roles they
          share (<Adr n="0210" />, <Path p={PERM} sym="def can(" />, <Path p={PERM} sym="def visible_teams(" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="What no token can do">
        <p>
          No token can approve a run, whatever its scopes, so an agent can't approve its own request
          (<Adr n="0050" />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/approvals.py" sym="Tokens can't, whatever their scopes" />).
          Workflow approvals and signatures that need a person signed in to the web app still need
          that sign-in (<Adr n="0210" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Scheduled triggers">
        <p>
          A scheduled trigger runs as a chosen service account, on the authority of the Admin who
          turned it on (<Adr n="0081" />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/models.py" sym="run_as = models.ForeignKey(" />).
          Triggers themselves are on <PageLink to="Workflows and triggers" />.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="MCP">
        <p>
          MCP tools call the REST API in the same process, as the agent, so a tool can do exactly what
          its endpoint allows. The agent's identity travels in an ASGI scope key, not a header, so no
          client can forge it. Requests from a foreign <code>Origin</code> are refused, and tool
          answers are fenced with a random marker (<Adr n={[13, 49]} />, <Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/mcp/views.py"
            sym="def _same_origin("
          />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/mcp/tools.py" sym="iki-data-" />, <Path
            p={SEC}
            sym='MCP_AGENT_KEY = "iki.mcp_agent"'
          />). The endpoint itself is on <PageLink to="Server and APIs" />.
        </p>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <div class="refs">
      <h3>Where</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Path p={`${OAUTH}/access.py`} />: what a token may do now</li>
        <li><Path p={`${OAUTH}/exchange.py`} />: token exchange for delegation</li>
        <li><Path p="server/src/iki_server/accounts/service_accounts.py" />: service accounts</li>
        <li><Path p="server/src/iki_server/mcp" />: the MCP endpoint and tools</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Decisions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Adr n="0013" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0027" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0028" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0049" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0072" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0073" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0152" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0210" title /></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
